course: everyday
lesson: 476
title: The secret that got told
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: At a sleepover Lily tells Maddie she has a crush on Kieran. «Pinky promise you won't tell.» By Monday lunchtime the whole class is singing «Lily and Kieran sitting in a tree». Maddie told «only Grace», and Grace told everyone. Lily doesn't speak to Maddie for a week. «Only one person» is exactly how everyone finds out.
explain: Secrets leak in sneaky ways. Sam tells Jake he still sleeps with a nightlight; Jake tells the football team for a laugh, and Sam is teased for a fortnight. Sam forgives him, but doesn't tell him anything private for a long time. Then Sam himself tells his big sister that Jake's parents are divorcing, thinking family doesn't count. She tells her best friend, whose little brother is in Sam's class. Jake knows within a week. Hana tells her mum that Chloe's parents are splitting up; her mum mentions it at the school gate, and soon the news has spread. At a sleepover Ellie reads out Maddie's diary, where Maddie had called her «a goose»; in the end both girls say sorry — Ellie for reading it, Maddie for the mean words. Gossip goes wrong too. Jake's mate tells him Sam called Jake's mum «just a cleaner»; Jake asks Sam straight, and Sam never said it — the mate made it up because he was jealous. And Grace tells the girls that Lily wets the bed. It's a lie about something private; Lily tells a teacher, and Grace has to say sorry.
explain: Some friends are a safe. Kieran tells Connor his dad lost his job and they might move. Connor keeps it for months: «Dunno, mate.» Later Kieran says, «You're the only one I trust.» Raj keeps Connor's crush secret for a whole year: «Took that to the grave, bro.» Small kept secrets are the bricks a friendship is built from.
explain: But one kind of secret must never be kept. Kayla shows Mia a bruise and says her stepdad did it. «Swear you won't tell. Swear it.» Mia swears, and can't sleep. First thing next morning she tells her teacher, and the school gets help for Kayla. Kayla is furious for a week, and months later she says thank you. If someone is being hurt, a promise to keep it secret doesn't count.
fact: A secret told in trust should stay secret; telling «just one person» is how secrets spread to everyone.
fact: Telling a friend's secret to family still breaks the secret; family members tell others too.
fact: Keeping a friend's secret is one of the main ways trust is built.
fact: After a secret is betrayed, trust takes a long time to come back; forgiving is not the same as forgetting.
fact: If someone is being hurt or is in danger, you must tell a trusted grown-up, even if you promised not to.
fact: Telling a grown-up about harm is not betraying a friend; it is getting them help.
fact: Family news, such as parents splitting up, is private and should not be passed on.
fact: Gossip is often untrue; check with the person directly before you react.
fact: Spreading a lie about something private, like bedwetting, is a kind of bullying; tell a teacher.
fact: Reading someone's diary is wrong, but mean words written down can still come out and hurt.
fact: A «pinky promise» is a children's promise made by hooking little fingers together.
exercise: Lily told Maddie about her crush. How did the whole class find out? | Maddie told «only Grace», who told everyone | Kieran guessed it himself from the way she looked | Lily told the class herself | The teacher read it out
exercise: What is a «pinky promise»? | A promise made by hooking little fingers | A promise written in pink pen | A promise you can break | A promise only for grown-ups
exercise: Jake told the team about Sam's nightlight. Did Sam forgive him? | Yes, but he stopped telling him private things for a while | Yes, and told him even more secrets the very next day | No, never, not even years later | He didn't notice at all
exercise: Hana told her mum about Chloe's parents. What happened? | The news spread | Nobody else ever heard about it | Chloe's parents got back together | Hana's mum kept it totally secret
exercise: Kieran's dad lost his job. What did Connor do with that secret? | Kept it for months | Told the lads straight away | Wrote it in the class chat | Told his teacher for a laugh
exercise: What did Kayla show Mia? | A bruise she blamed on her stepdad | A new phone she got for Christmas from Nan | A secret diary full of poems | A note from a boy in Year 6
exercise: What did Mia do about Kayla's bruise? | Told her teacher first thing next morning | Kept the promise and said nothing to anyone | Told the whole class | Asked Kayla to show everyone
exercise: Why was Mia right to break her promise? | Someone being hurt needs a grown-up's help | Promises never matter at all, so you can break any | Mia wanted Kayla in trouble | Kayla was lying anyway
exercise: How did Kayla feel about Mia telling, over time? | Furious for a week, then thankful months later | Furious forever, and she never spoke to Mia again | Happy from the very first minute | She never found out
exercise: Ellie read out Maddie's diary. Who apologised? | Both girls | Only Ellie, for reading it | Only Maddie, for writing it | Neither of them did
exercise: Jake's mate said Sam called Jake's mum a cleaner. What was the truth? | The mate made it up because he was jealous | Sam really said it, but only as a joke at lunch | Jake's mum told everyone | A teacher said it
exercise: Grace told the girls Lily wets the bed. Is it true? | No, it was a lie about something private | Yes, Grace saw it herself at a sleepover | Yes, Lily told her | Nobody knows
exercise: What did Raj do with Connor's crush secret? | Kept it a whole year | Told the girl the next day | Posted it in the group chat | Told his own sister
exercise: Sam told his sister Jake's secret. Why was that still wrong? | His sister told others, and it got back to Jake | It wasn't wrong, because family doesn't count at all | Sisters always tell teachers | It's only wrong with brothers
exercise: What should you do before reacting to gossip about what someone said? | Ask the person directly | Shout at them in front of everyone | Spread it further | Believe it straight away
exercise: What builds trust between friends? | Keeping their secrets, small and big | Telling everyone they're your friend | Buying them presents | Sitting next to them
test: «I only told one person, so it's basically still a secret». Is it? | No, «one person» is how it spreads | Yes, one person doesn't count | Yes, if they're your best friend in the world | No, but only if it's two people
test: Kayla: «swear on ur life u won't tell». She's being hurt at home. What should Mia do? | Tell a trusted grown-up anyway | Keep the promise no matter what | Tell the kids in class | Try to sort it out herself
test: «she'll hate me if I tell». What does the lesson show about that worry? | She may be cross at first but she'll be safer | She'll hate you forever, so it's best not to tell | She'll be happy at once | It doesn't matter if she's hurt
test: «it's my sister, family don't count, innit». Is telling family OK? | No, it can still spread back to your friend | Yes, family can never pass things on to anyone else | Yes, sisters are like diaries | No, only tell your cousins
test: «my mate says Sam's been dissing my mum, I'm gonna batter him». (dissing means saying rude things.) Best move? | Ask Sam directly | Batter him straight away | Tell everyone Sam's a liar | Spread a rumour back about him
test: «spill the tea, why's Chloe sad, is it her parents?» (spill the tea means tell the gossip.) What should Hana say? | It's private, I'm not saying | Yeah, they're splitting up, tell everyone | Make up a juicier story | Post it in the chat
test: What would probably happen if Connor had told the lads about Kieran's dad's job? | Kieran would feel betrayed and stop trusting him | Kieran would be grateful | Nothing, nobody would care | Kieran's dad would get his job back sooner with their help
test: «Ellie read your diary out, but you did call her a goose». Who was in the wrong? | Both: reading it was wrong and so were the mean words | Only Ellie, because diaries are private, whatever's in them | Only Maddie, she wrote it | Nobody, it was a sleepover
test: «Grace says Lily wets the bed lol». Lily knows it's a lie. What should she do? | Tell a teacher, lies like that are bullying | Laugh along so it stops | Start a worse rumour about Grace to get her back | Never come back to school
test: In a film, the hero keeps a friend's dangerous secret to the end and it all works out. What's true in real life? | If someone's being hurt, tell a grown-up | Dangerous secrets are best kept | Grown-ups can't help with secrets | Only tell once it's too late
test: «Took that to the grave, bro». What does Raj mean? | He kept the secret | He buried something in the garden | He forgot all about it in the end | He told everyone at a funeral
exam: Ascertain the conduct required of a child to whom a peer has disclosed, under a promise of secrecy, that she is being physically harmed at home. | To inform a trusted adult notwithstanding the promise | To preserve the secret in all circumstances | To disclose the matter to fellow pupils | To attempt to resolve the matter unaided
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the disclosure of a confidence to «only one person»? | It is the customary means by which a confidence becomes generally known | It preserves the confidence in its entirety | It is permitted where that person is a relative | It renders the original confidant blameless
exam: Identify the principal means, as established in the lesson, by which trust between friends is established. | The consistent keeping of each other's confidences | The giving of costly presents on every possible occasion | Public declarations of friendship | Proximity in the classroom
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a pupil who receives a report that a friend has insulted his mother. | He ought to verify the report directly with the friend before reacting | He ought to retaliate without delay, lest the insult be thought acceptable | He ought to circulate the report further | He ought to accept the report as true
